From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Subject: net: xpon: omci: GEM port PM history from the MAC counters

The Movistar Huawei OLT creates a GEM port network CTP performance
monitoring history data ME (G.988 9.2.13, class 341) for each GEM port
network CTP and reads it every 15 minutes. The agent answered from an
opaque MIB shadow: zeros, with the threshold data ID echoed in the bytes
of the interval end time.

Fill it from the per-GEM MAC counters (patches 994/996):

- omci_device_ops gains get_gem_counters(), which returns the 64-bit
  frame and payload byte totals of a batch of GEM ports plus a generation
  that changes whenever the totals restart from zero (GEM port removed and
  added again, MAC reset). The Airoha MAC folds its MIB in under
  gem_mib_lock, a mutex, so the op may sleep; the agent only calls it
  from process context with its own lock held, the order the agent
  already uses for set_gem_port().

- A 15-minute interval clock (G.988 I.4) in the agent: a delayed work
  samples every minute and closes the interval on time; a Get on ME 341
  also closes an interval whose end has passed, so the OLT never reads a
  stale one. Synchronize time restarts the clock at interval 0 and clears
  every counter. Before the first Synchronize time the clock runs from
  agent start, so the OLT gets real counts either way.

- When an interval closes, the counts of each instance become its history
  attributes and the interval end time steps (modulo 256). An instance
  created later takes the current interval number and counts from its
  creation. The ME ID is that of the CTP, whose Port-ID names the GEM
  port; transmitted is upstream, received downstream. The 32-bit frame
  counters saturate. Encryption key errors is G.987 only and stays 0.
  The threshold data 1/2 ID is the only writable attribute and is kept.

- Get returns the last completed interval, Get current data the running
  counts. Baseline responses carry 25 attribute bytes; what does not fit
  is reported with result 9 and the attribute execution mask, extended
  responses put both failure masks before the values.

ME 341 gets a descriptor, appended last so that the Attribute ME IDs of
the other classes do not move. Every other ME is answered as before.

/sys/class/xpon/<netdev>/omci/gem_pm (pon0 on the XR500v) shows the
clock and, per instance, the GEM port, the history attributes and the
running counts.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
